# =============================================================================
# Lake Effect Helm — environment template
# Copy to .env.local. NEVER commit a populated copy.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database connections
#
# Helm uses FOUR distinct Postgres roles. They are separate roles, not separate
# servers — the separation exists so that a bug or an injection in one subsystem
# cannot reach another subsystem's data.
#
#   helm_app       request-scoped application traffic. RLS-constrained, cannot
#                  read secret ciphertext directly, cannot read auth tokens.
#   helm_auth      Auth.js adapter only. Owns the auth_* tables. No access to
#                  tenant data.
#   helm_key_admin key rotation / KMS bridge job. Only role that may write
#                  tenant_data_key.
#   helm_auditor   read-only compliance access to audit history.
#   helm_worker    background jobs. helm_app's privileges plus tenant
#                  enumeration; never used by the web tier.
#   helm_migrator  DDL owner, used by `pnpm db:migrate`. Never used at runtime.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://helm_app:CHANGE_ME@localhost:5432/helm?sslmode=require"
DATABASE_URL_AUTH="postgresql://helm_auth:CHANGE_ME@localhost:5432/helm?sslmode=require"
DATABASE_URL_KEY_ADMIN="postgresql://helm_key_admin:CHANGE_ME@localhost:5432/helm?sslmode=require"
DATABASE_URL_AUDITOR="postgresql://helm_auditor:CHANGE_ME@localhost:5432/helm?sslmode=require"
# MIGRATIONS MUST RUN AS A ROLE THAT ROW-LEVEL SECURITY DOES NOT APPLY TO —
# in practice a superuser. Not a style preference: every table here is FORCE
# ROW LEVEL SECURITY, which subjects the table OWNER to its own policies, and
# every SECURITY DEFINER function runs as whoever owns it. Migrate as a role
# RLS applies to and those functions match zero rows — helm.reveal_secret()
# returns NULL instead of the credential, with no error anywhere. The runner
# refuses rather than letting that happen; see db/migrate.ts.
#
# This connection is used by `pnpm db:migrate` and by nothing at runtime. The
# five roles below are the ones the application actually uses, and none of
# them may be a superuser or hold BYPASSRLS.
DATABASE_URL_MIGRATOR="postgresql://postgres:CHANGE_ME@localhost:5432/helm?sslmode=require"
# The background worker. A MEMBER of helm_app — same tables, same RLS — plus
# EXECUTE on the cross-tenant backlog enumerators, which helm_app must never
# hold because anything the request role can execute is reachable from HTTP.
DATABASE_URL_WORKER="postgresql://helm_worker:CHANGE_ME@localhost:5432/helm?sslmode=require"

# The migration runner also accepts the standard PG* variables (PGHOST, PGPORT,
# PGUSER, PGDATABASE) when no URL is set — needed for unix sockets, which
# postgres.js does not honour inside a URL query string.

# Pool sizing. Keep app pool modest: every request takes a connection for the
# duration of its transaction because RLS context is set with SET LOCAL.
DATABASE_POOL_MAX="20"
DATABASE_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS="15000"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Key hierarchy  (see docs/architecture/03-crypto-operations.md)
#
#   KEK  (Key Encryption Key)   the master key. On-premises this is either
#                               HashiCorp Vault's transit engine, or a key file
#                               held by this host.
#    └─ DEK (Data Encryption Key), one per tenant, stored wrapped in
#       tenant_data_key.wrapped_dek, unwrapped on demand and cached in memory
#        └─ AES-256-GCM field ciphertext in secret_version
#
# Helm is deployed on-premises and does NOT use a cloud KMS. Two providers are
# supported in production:
#
#   vault-transit   Preferred. The master key never enters the Helm process.
#                   Every wrap and unwrap is a Vault API call recorded in its
#                   audit device, and revoking Helm's Vault token stops
#                   decryption immediately — you have a kill switch.
#
#   local-keyfile   Simpler: no second service to keep alive. The master key is
#                   held by this host, so root on this box can decrypt every
#                   secret in the database and there is no kill switch and no
#                   record of what was decrypted. That is an acceptable trade
#                   for many single-server MSP deployments — make it knowingly.
#
# local-dev is refused when NODE_ENV=production.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One of: vault-transit | local-keyfile | local-dev
#         (aws-kms | gcp-kms | azure-keyvault are recognised but not wired)
HELM_KEK_PROVIDER="local-keyfile"

# --- local-keyfile -----------------------------------------------------------
#
# HELM_KEK_FILE is strongly preferred over HELM_KEK_B64. An environment variable
# is copied into crash dumps, `docker inspect`, the unit file that set it, the
# CI system that rendered that file, and /proc/<pid>/environ.
#
# The file must not be readable by group or other — Helm refuses to start
# otherwise. Note `docker secret` mounts at 0444 by default; set `mode: 0400`.
# With systemd, prefer:
#
#   [Service]
#   LoadCredential=helm-kek:/etc/helm/master.key
#   Environment=HELM_KEK_FILE=%d/helm-kek
#
# Create a key ring:
#
#   umask 077
#   printf '{"current":"v1","keys":{"v1":"%s"}}\n' "$(openssl rand -base64 32)" \
#     > /etc/helm/master.key
#   chmod 0400 /etc/helm/master.key
#
# A file containing only a bare base64 key is also accepted and treated as "v1".
#
# ROTATION. The key ring is versioned because replacing a single key would make
# every existing tenant DEK permanently unopenable. To rotate: add a new version
# to "keys", point "current" at it, restart, then re-wrap the tenant DEKs with
# `pnpm helm:rotate-kek` before removing the old version. Removing a version
# that still wraps live DEKs is unrecoverable data loss.
HELM_KEK_FILE="/etc/helm/master.key"
HELM_KEK_B64=""

# Names this key ring in tenant_data_key.kek_id as "<label>/<version>". Changing
# it after keys exist makes their kek_id unresolvable.
HELM_KEK_ID="helm-master"

# --- vault-transit -----------------------------------------------------------
#
# The transit key MUST be created with derived=true. Without derivation Vault
# silently ignores the per-tenant encryption context, and a DEK wrapped for one
# client would open as another's:
#
#   vault secrets enable transit
#   vault write -f transit/keys/helm-tenant-kek type=aes256-gcm96 derived=true
#
# Helm refuses to start against a non-derived key rather than trusting this
# comment to have been read.
#
# Policy Helm needs, and nothing more — note no `encrypt` and no key writes:
#
#   path "transit/datakey/plaintext/helm-tenant-kek" { capabilities = ["update"] }
#   path "transit/decrypt/helm-tenant-kek"           { capabilities = ["update"] }
#   path "transit/keys/helm-tenant-kek"              { capabilities = ["read"] }
#
# For a private CA, point NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS at the bundle. Never disable TLS
# verification: the Vault token and every unwrapped DEK cross this connection.
VAULT_ADDR="https://vault.internal:8200"
HELM_VAULT_TRANSIT_MOUNT="transit"
HELM_VAULT_TRANSIT_KEY="helm-tenant-kek"
HELM_VAULT_TIMEOUT_MS="5000"
VAULT_NAMESPACE=""

# AppRole is the right choice for a long-running server: the token renews
# itself, and the SecretID can be response-wrapped and short-lived.
VAULT_APPROLE_MOUNT="approle"
VAULT_ROLE_ID=""
VAULT_SECRET_ID=""

# Static token. Simple, but it expires with no way to renew, and it sits in the
# environment. Use for one-shot jobs, not for the server.
VAULT_TOKEN=""

# --- local-dev ONLY ----------------------------------------------------------
# 32 raw bytes, base64: openssl rand -base64 32
# Unversioned, so the master key can never be rotated. Development and CI only.
HELM_LOCAL_KEK_B64=""

# How long an unwrapped DEK may stay in the process memory cache. Keep this
# short under vault-transit: the cache is what bounds how long a compromise of
# the Helm process yields plaintext keys after the Vault token is revoked.
HELM_DEK_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS="300"

# Separate key used to derive per-tenant blind-index HMACs for password-reuse
# detection. Must NOT be derivable from the KEK. 32 bytes, base64.
HELM_BLIND_INDEX_KEY_B64=""

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Auth (Auth.js v5)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
AUTH_SECRET=""
AUTH_URL="https://helm.example.com"
AUTH_TRUST_HOST="false"

# How many entries your infrastructure appends to X-Forwarded-For. The client
# address is read that many places from the RIGHT — the leftmost entry is
# entirely client-controlled, and trusting it defeats API token IP allowlisting.
HELM_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS="1"

# DEVELOPMENT ONLY. Set to an app_user email to bypass authentication entirely
# and run every request as that user. Helm REFUSES TO START with this set and
# NODE_ENV=production. It resolves a real user row and grants nothing — role,
# scope and permissions still come from the membership — but it is an
# authentication bypass and is named like one.
HELM_DEV_SESSION_EMAIL=""

# Optional SSO providers. Leave blank to disable.
AUTH_MICROSOFT_ENTRA_ID_ID=""
AUTH_MICROSOFT_ENTRA_ID_SECRET=""
AUTH_MICROSOFT_ENTRA_ID_ISSUER=""

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local passwords
#
# Nothing here is required. Local sign-in works out of the box and is what gets
# you in when Entra cannot be reached — which, since an MSP's identity provider
# and its mail are usually the same outage, is exactly when you need a client's
# credentials most.
#
# Hashing parameters, length floor, lockout thresholds and the rate limits are
# NOT configurable. They are in src/lib/auth/password.ts and
# db/sql/0340_local_authentication.sql, where they can be reasoned about, rather
# than in an environment file where a deployment can quietly weaken them.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where a SELF-SERVICE reset email is handed off. POSTed as
# {to, subject, text}; must be https, or http on the loopback address, because
# the body contains a working reset link.
#
# LEAVE THIS BLANK unless you have somewhere for it to go. With it unset, the
# self-service endpoint REFUSES rather than accepting the request and dropping
# the mail — "check your email" when nothing was sent is worse than an error.
#
# It is not needed for the path that matters: an administrator issues a reset
# code and reads it to the person. That works with no mail server at all, which
# is the point.
HELM_RESET_DELIVERY_URL=""

# Minutes a step-up (re-auth / WebAuthn) verification stays valid before a
# high-sensitivity secret reveal requires it again.
HELM_STEP_UP_TTL_MINUTES="15"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Background workers  (pnpm helm:worker)
#
# Mutual exclusion between worker instances is a POSTGRES ADVISORY LOCK, not a
# Redis lock. Two app servers behind a load balancer both run the runtime, and
# two concurrent syncs against one connection is how duplicate assets get
# created — so correctness must not depend on a second service that nobody on an
# on-premises deployment is monitoring. Redis is optional and buys throughput,
# not correctness.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
REDIS_URL="redis://localhost:6379"

# debug | info | warn | error. Worker logs are one JSON object per line.
HELM_LOG_LEVEL="info"

# Attribution for maintenance jobs run from a shell (pnpm helm:rotate-kek).
# Every key operation is recorded against an actor; this is which one.
HELM_MAINTENANCE_ACTOR_ID=""

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Search
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# postgres | meilisearch  — Postgres FTS is the default and the only backend
# permitted to index secret *metadata*. Meilisearch, if enabled, receives
# non-sensitive documents only.
HELM_SEARCH_BACKEND="postgres"
MEILISEARCH_HOST=""
MEILISEARCH_API_KEY=""

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Audit
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Days of audit_log retained online before partitions are detached to cold
# storage. Compliance floor for most MSP contracts is 365.
HELM_AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS="2555"

# Optional append-only mirror (S3 Object Lock / WORM bucket) for the audit chain.
HELM_AUDIT_MIRROR_BUCKET=""

# Where the anchoring worker writes witnessed chain heads, one JSON line each.
#
# The hash chain proves nobody edited history without rewriting everything
# after it. It does NOT prove the whole chain was not rewritten by someone with
# database ownership — which, on-premises, is the MSP's own staff. Anchoring
# closes that, but only to the extent that this directory is somewhere Helm
# cannot rewrite: a WORM mount, an append-only share, a path your backup system
# snapshots. A directory the Helm process can also rewrite is not a witness.
#
# Unset, the worker verifies the chain and logs the head but does NOT record an
# anchor — claiming an anchor that does not exist is the failure this is
# guarding against.
HELM_AUDIT_ANCHOR_DIR=""

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compliance and offboarding exports
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where rendered bundles are stored. Files are written mode 0600. Use a
# directory on encrypted storage; an expired handover archive still sitting on
# disk is the breach the TTL exists to prevent, and the expiry worker can only
# delete what it can reach.
HELM_EXPORT_DIR="/var/lib/helm/exports"

# Where the passphrase for a credential-bearing bundle is delivered.
#
# The bundle is encrypted under a passphrase generated at render time and stored
# NOWHERE — not in the database, not in the audit log. That is what makes the
# artefact at rest useless to anyone holding only the file. But the render
# happens in a worker with nobody watching, so the passphrase needs somewhere to
# go: one mode-0600 file per export, for an operator to collect and hand over
# out of band.
#
# Put this on a DIFFERENT backup set from HELM_EXPORT_DIR. The whole scheme
# rests on the bundle and its passphrase not travelling together.
#
# Unset, the worker REFUSES to render a credential-bearing export rather than
# producing a file nobody can open.
HELM_EXPORT_PASSPHRASE_DIR=""
